- `action(n1, "Start process")`: Hallucinates a dedicated initialization step not present in the source, which begins directly with item return/pickup activities. (SMC1)
- `defines(v1, ...)`: Value range only contains `"Delivery crew picks up items at event site"`, omitting the `"Customer returns the items to the store"` option explicitly detailed in the source. (SMC6)
- `defines(v3, ...)` & `defines(v6, ...)`: Value ranges only contain `"Stored for future orders"`, omitting the `"packed for an existing order"` alternative explicitly detailed in the source. (SMC6)
- `defines(v4, ...)`: Value range only contains `"Customer does not return missing items"`, omitting the `"Customer returns the missing items"` condition explicitly detailed in the source. (SMC6)
- `branch(b1, XOR)`, `branch(b3, XOR)`, `branch(b4, XOR)`, `branch(b6, XOR)`: Each declares an exclusive choice but defines only a single outgoing `precedence` edge, failing to structurally represent the alternative pathways described in the text. (SMC4)
- `guard(b1, n3, v1 = 'Delivery crew picks up items at event site')`: Restricts flow continuation solely to the delivery crew scenario, contradicting the source which states both customer return and delivery pickup proceed to the counting step. (SMC1, SMC4)
- `precedence(n13, c1)`: Routes the discarded items path (`action(n13)`) into `converge(c1)` and onward to disposition (`action(n14)`), incorrectly implying discarded items undergo further packing/storage. The source only specifies emailing replacement charges for discarded items. (SMC1, SMC5)
- `guard(b3, n15, v3 = 'Stored for future orders')` & `guard(b6, n16, v6 = 'Stored for future orders')`: Guards only validate the storage condition, ignoring the packing alternative, thereby forcing an incomplete representation of the intended disposition logic. (SMC4)

